title: 'In the reader: a note is understood in one read where it fits what pretraining already holds.'
pid: 99257530-48cb-484d-8e95-959e02bb174a
body: |2-
  A claim in common words is learned by reading it; a name coined in
  one session ("the seven principles") fits no schema the reader has.
  This is why Jeff asks Libby to "use a common language, idioms", why
  a link titled with a claim is followed and one titled with a topic's
  name is not (Ed's finding), and why agents use well the tools their
  training saw: Letta's agents, given only grep, search and open over
  plain files, answered 74% of a memory benchmark's questions, above
  the 68.5% reported for a graph memory (Letta's own figures, 2025).
  So skg-for-ai is right to show the graph as Org files.
